{"skill":{"slug":"tmux","displayName":"Tmux"},"version":{"version":"1.0.0","createdAt":1767545387948,"changelog":"","changelogSource":null,"license":null,"files":[{"path":"scripts/wait-for-text.sh","size":2139,"sha256":"96bc42702f7353c32233c223d61dd11f2f99033267aeefac838aea8983a67735","contentType":"application/x-sh"},{"path":"scripts/find-sessions.sh","size":2945,"sha256":"a137d03b3b37332439a1592831d60479ff86315c7e6d6ed7da0fb799db0f13f7","contentType":"application/x-sh"},{"path":"SKILL.md","size":4112,"sha256":"9801a7dde4106af5ec10c261cf4335cc6bec1cf98760f25829353d40f87906e6","contentType":"text/markdown"},{"path":"skill-card.md","size":2100,"sha256":"21db8901f3be9d4076a3e9f9502decfe7758bcf4314ab420db3d28459e00d8f0","contentType":"text/markdown"}],"security":{"status":"suspicious","hasWarnings":true,"checkedAt":1789013665110,"model":null,"hasScanResult":true,"sha256hash":"4b63a6c51996791aa5b629c95466a6fa29e7d769c4d7107432dbdaafe5be9bde","virustotalUrl":"https://www.virustotal.com/gui/file/4b63a6c51996791aa5b629c95466a6fa29e7d769c4d7107432dbdaafe5be9bde","scanners":{"vt":{"status":"clean","verdict":"benign","normalizedStatus":"clean","analysis":"Type: OpenClaw Skill\nName: tmux\nVersion: 1.0.0\n\nThe OpenClaw AgentSkills skill bundle for 'tmux' is classified as benign. Its purpose is to provide an interface for an AI agent to interact with `tmux` sessions, enabling control over interactive CLIs. The `SKILL.md` provides clear instructions and examples for using `tmux` commands, creating sessions, sending input, and capturing output, all consistent with its stated purpose. The helper scripts (`find-sessions.sh`, `wait-for-text.sh`) are straightforward shell scripts that wrap `tmux` commands and do not exhibit any malicious behavior, data exfiltration, or obfuscation. There is no evidence of prompt injection attempts against the agent to deviate from its task or perform unauthorized actions, nor any other indicators of intentional harmful behavior.","source":"palm","checkedAt":1779159627859},"skillspector":{"status":"clean","normalizedStatus":"clean","score":18,"severity":"LOW","recommendation":"SAFE","issueCount":2,"checkedAt":1789013665110},"llm":{"status":"suspicious","verdict":"suspicious","normalizedStatus":"suspicious","confidence":"high","summary":"The skill is mostly a coherent tmux automation helper, but it recommends unattended high-authority coding-agent execution and can print captured terminal contents into logs on timeout.","dimensions":[{"detail":"Requiring tmux on macOS/Linux is proportionate for the purpose, but capturing up to 1000 pane-history lines by default can expose sensitive terminal data beyond what a user may expect from a failed wait.","label":"Credentials","name":"environment_proportionality","rating":"note"},{"detail":"The artifact contains a markdown skill and two local shell helper scripts, with no package install hook, remote payload retrieval, tool replacement, or hidden installer behavior found.","label":"Install Mechanism","name":"install_mechanism","rating":"ok"},{"detail":"Instructions are mostly scoped to private tmux sockets and explicit targets, but the autonomous-agent section normalizes non-interactive fixes and the wait helper prints pane contents on timeout without an opt-in debug control.","label":"Instruction Scope","name":"instruction_scope","rating":"concern"},{"detail":"The skill creates and controls tmux sessions and sockets and documents cleanup commands, but it does not install services, scheduled tasks, shell hooks, persistent memory, or privilege-escalation mechanisms.","label":"Persistence & Privilege","name":"persistence_privilege","rating":"note"},{"detail":"The stated purpose, remote-controlling tmux sessions by sending keys and scraping pane output, matches most capabilities; however, the documented Codex orchestration examples recommend `--yolo`/`--full-auto`, which expands the practical impact from tmux control to unattended code and shell mutation without clear safety boundaries.","label":"Purpose & Capability","name":"purpose_capability","rating":"concern"}],"guidance":"Install only if you are comfortable with a tmux helper that can drive interactive sessions and read pane output. Avoid using the documented `--yolo` or `--full-auto` examples except in isolated worktrees or sandboxes, and be careful using `wait-for-text.sh` around panes that may contain tokens, credentials, private source, or other sensitive output because timeouts print pane history to stderr.","findings":"[SQP-2] unexpected: The finding about `codex --yolo`/`--full-auto` is supported by SKILL.md lines 80-101. Running coding agents in tmux is purpose-related, but recommending unattended approval-bypassing modes without guardrails is overbroad for a general tmux helper.\n[SQP-2] expected: The finding about timeout pane dumping is supported by scripts/wait-for-text.sh lines 75-79. Capturing pane output is expected for this skill, but automatically writing the captured contents to stderr creates a real under-disclosed logging risk.\n[T05] unexpected: The SARIF concern is supported by the documented `codex --yolo` examples and the statement that Codex needs `--yolo` or `--full-auto` for non-interactive fixes; this is not malicious by itself, but it justifies Review because it weakens user approval boundaries.\n[T09] expected: The SARIF concern is supported by the wait helper printing captured pane history on timeout; the behavior is diagnostic and local, but potentially exposes secrets or private command output to logs.","agenticRiskFindings":null,"riskSummary":null,"model":null,"checkedAt":1789013665110}}}}}